WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:05.920
Most people treat Chat GPT like a new

00:00:02.960 --> 00:00:08.960
intern, but I use it to try five

00:00:05.920 --> 00:00:12.760
strategies. It's like having five of the

00:00:08.960 --> 00:00:16.000
world's smartest minds work 24/7 for

00:00:12.760 --> 00:00:18.720
only $20 a month to help me move faster,

00:00:16.000 --> 00:00:21.120
decide better, and focus on what truly

00:00:18.720 --> 00:00:24.200
matters. But, I only figured this out

00:00:21.120 --> 00:00:26.855
after spending years in tech and AI as a

00:00:24.200 --> 00:00:26.880
CEO, board member, and investor,

00:00:26.855 --> 00:00:29.520
>> [music]

00:00:26.880 --> 00:00:32.680
>> where I watched elite teams use AI in

00:00:29.520 --> 00:00:35.240
ways most people never see. So, today

00:00:32.680 --> 00:00:38.640
I'm sharing those five strategies to use

00:00:35.240 --> 00:00:41.720
Chat GPT so well, it feels illegal. The

00:00:38.640 --> 00:00:44.540
first strategy is called the clone. When

00:00:41.720 --> 00:00:44.640
I was a kid, my grandpa had

00:00:44.540 --> 00:00:47.040
>> [music]

00:00:44.640 --> 00:00:48.840
>> late-stage Alzheimer's. Every few

00:00:47.040 --> 00:00:51.000
minutes, he would look at my

00:00:48.840 --> 00:00:53.880
grandmother, the woman he'd been married

00:00:51.000 --> 00:00:55.720
for over 60 years, and he would ask,

00:00:53.880 --> 00:00:58.000
"Who are you?" [music] And then he would

00:00:55.720 --> 00:00:59.840
go ahead and tell me a story, forgetting

00:00:58.000 --> 00:01:02.480
that he just told [music] me the exact

00:00:59.840 --> 00:01:05.040
same story 5 minutes ago. His brain

00:01:02.480 --> 00:01:08.280
didn't have memory of his last 5

00:01:05.040 --> 00:01:10.600
minutes. Chat GPT suffers from something

00:01:08.280 --> 00:01:13.600
very similar. Every time you open a new

00:01:10.600 --> 00:01:16.560
chat session, you're starting from zero

00:01:13.600 --> 00:01:20.120
context. But, there is a way to give

00:01:16.560 --> 00:01:23.040
Chat GPT an almost telepathic ability.

00:01:20.120 --> 00:01:26.000
It can keep the memory and context for

00:01:23.040 --> 00:01:28.440
you alive without you explaining every

00:01:26.000 --> 00:01:32.720
single time. For that, I apply a

00:01:28.440 --> 00:01:36.200
three-step framework called ESP. E is

00:01:32.720 --> 00:01:37.440
extract. Open the voice mode in Chat GPT

00:01:36.200 --> 00:01:39.880
and say,

00:01:37.440 --> 00:01:42.680
"Listen, you're my chief of staff.

00:01:39.880 --> 00:01:45.680
Interview me for 20 minutes, 30 minutes,

00:01:42.680 --> 00:01:47.200
my background, my tone, my strategy, my

00:01:45.680 --> 00:01:50.640
blind spots."

00:01:47.200 --> 00:01:54.040
Then, talk a while. It be messy. Don't

00:01:50.640 --> 00:01:58.040
self-edit. This step is about raw

00:01:54.040 --> 00:02:00.800
capture only. S is synthesize. Now, this

00:01:58.040 --> 00:02:02.640
is where you turn the raw data into an

00:02:00.800 --> 00:02:04.640
operating system. So, upload the

00:02:02.640 --> 00:02:08.080
transcript of whatever you said to

00:02:04.640 --> 00:02:10.640
ChatGPT and ask ChatGPT to organize it

00:02:08.080 --> 00:02:13.160
into a summary on who you are and how

00:02:10.640 --> 00:02:15.760
you think. Now, go to settings, custom

00:02:13.160 --> 00:02:17.760
instructions, and paste your summary. By

00:02:15.760 --> 00:02:20.480
the way, this is also the place where

00:02:17.760 --> 00:02:23.320
you tell ChatGPT exactly how you would

00:02:20.480 --> 00:02:26.560
want it to behave. Always format data as

00:02:23.320 --> 00:02:29.880
tables. Don't send me walls of text. But

00:02:26.560 --> 00:02:32.720
now, ChatGPT knows who you are, how you

00:02:29.880 --> 00:02:35.880
want your responses shaped, and what to

00:02:32.720 --> 00:02:38.560
avoid by default. And here's where it

00:02:35.880 --> 00:02:41.600
gets really wild. P is for [music]

00:02:38.560 --> 00:02:44.480
projects. Think of a project as a space

00:02:41.600 --> 00:02:47.959
where you store your context. It lives

00:02:44.480 --> 00:02:50.720
with your instructions, memories, files,

00:02:47.959 --> 00:02:54.240
repeated tasks. For instance, for your

00:02:50.720 --> 00:02:56.280
work projects, upload your resume, PDFs,

00:02:54.240 --> 00:02:58.840
and data that's relevant. You can even

00:02:56.280 --> 00:03:01.720
customize the instructions inside each

00:02:58.840 --> 00:03:04.920
project settings. Just remember not to

00:03:01.720 --> 00:03:07.480
mix domains. So, for example, I have my

00:03:04.920 --> 00:03:10.000
consulting project and then my YouTube

00:03:07.480 --> 00:03:12.640
project [music] and my health project

00:03:10.000 --> 00:03:16.280
separate. This turns your AI from a

00:03:12.640 --> 00:03:18.600
temporary chat into a persistent brain.

00:03:16.280 --> 00:03:20.640
But that's only the first battle. You

00:03:18.600 --> 00:03:23.600
still need an army to do the heavy

00:03:20.640 --> 00:03:26.040
lifting. That is our next strategy. The

00:03:23.600 --> 00:03:28.292
second strategy is called the swarm.

00:03:26.040 --> 00:03:28.320
There's a great story in ancient

00:03:28.292 --> 00:03:31.239
>> [music]

00:03:28.320 --> 00:03:33.519
>> Indian mythology about a demon who was

00:03:31.239 --> 00:03:36.920
impossible to defeat with a single

00:03:33.519 --> 00:03:39.560
weapon. So, the great goddess manifested

00:03:36.920 --> 00:03:42.840
eight arms wielding eight different

00:03:39.560 --> 00:03:45.920
weapons at once. She was known as

00:03:42.840 --> 00:03:48.320
Ashtabhuja Durga. Eight arms, eight

00:03:45.920 --> 00:03:49.519
weapons, eight battles fought [music] in

00:03:48.320 --> 00:03:51.880
parallel.

00:03:49.519 --> 00:03:54.760
And most of us are fighting our own

00:03:51.880 --> 00:03:57.520
modern demons with just our two bare

00:03:54.760 --> 00:04:00.760
hands. Our brain can only handle one

00:03:57.520 --> 00:04:03.120
task at a time. You need to hire three

00:04:00.760 --> 00:04:07.000
specific advanced capabilities of

00:04:03.120 --> 00:04:09.600
ChatGPT. Number one, your hands. Stop

00:04:07.000 --> 00:04:12.160
doing tasks one by one. Use the agent

00:04:09.600 --> 00:04:14.560
mode. Dispatch the swarm. Instead of

00:04:12.160 --> 00:04:17.079
searching for a single lead, give

00:04:14.560 --> 00:04:20.320
ChatGPT a command. [music] Find 50 leads

00:04:17.079 --> 00:04:22.720
in the SaaS space. Verify their emails.

00:04:20.320 --> 00:04:25.278
Rank them by revenue. That's it. Let it

00:04:22.720 --> 00:04:27.840
run. You go get coffee. But there is one

00:04:25.278 --> 00:04:30.919
[music] catch. ChatGPT is still trapped

00:04:27.840 --> 00:04:33.080
inside a text box. It can find leads,

00:04:30.919 --> 00:04:35.880
but it can't actually put them into your

00:04:33.080 --> 00:04:38.160
CRM or build a process around them.

00:04:35.880 --> 00:04:41.240
Number two, your eyes.

00:04:38.160 --> 00:04:43.880
Stop describing your problems. ChatGPT

00:04:41.240 --> 00:04:46.760
is very visual. You can take a photo of

00:04:43.880 --> 00:04:49.880
a whiteboard sketch, a broken car part,

00:04:46.760 --> 00:04:52.040
or a complex analytics dashboard. Just

00:04:49.880 --> 00:04:53.760
upload it and then say, "Analyze this

00:04:52.040 --> 00:04:55.880
picture. Tell me what's wrong with it

00:04:53.760 --> 00:04:58.360
and tell me one move that I can make to

00:04:55.880 --> 00:05:00.120
fix it." You're using ChatGPT as your

00:04:58.360 --> 00:05:02.560
eyes, not just your keyboard. [music]

00:05:00.120 --> 00:05:04.840
And the great thing is ChatGPT can see

00:05:02.560 --> 00:05:07.080
things human eyes can't. And finally,

00:05:04.840 --> 00:05:09.240
number three, your research assistant.

00:05:07.080 --> 00:05:11.640
You can do deep research that was once

00:05:09.240 --> 00:05:14.200
the domain of an analyst who you would

00:05:11.640 --> 00:05:16.280
pay $50,000 for. Recently, I was in a

00:05:14.200 --> 00:05:18.960
meeting with a chief technology officer

00:05:16.280 --> 00:05:21.080
of a large firm and he said something

00:05:18.960 --> 00:05:23.240
very interesting. He said, "I had to

00:05:21.080 --> 00:05:25.840
evaluate six [music] presentations

00:05:23.240 --> 00:05:28.240
delivered by a very large IT consulting

00:05:25.840 --> 00:05:30.160
firm on the technology infrastructure of

00:05:28.240 --> 00:05:32.640
a massive data [music] center." And

00:05:30.160 --> 00:05:35.160
there were 300 pages of dense

00:05:32.640 --> 00:05:37.840
information. But what did he do? He

00:05:35.160 --> 00:05:41.120
uploaded them all in ChatGPT, hired a

00:05:37.840 --> 00:05:43.440
swarm of AI analysts one afternoon.

00:05:41.120 --> 00:05:45.188
That's all it took. Now, that would have

00:05:43.440 --> 00:05:45.640
taken weeks before.

00:05:45.188 --> 00:05:47.720
>> [music]

00:05:45.640 --> 00:05:50.280
>> That's the world we're heading into. In

00:05:47.720 --> 00:05:53.000
the age of AI, you won't be billing for

00:05:50.280 --> 00:05:55.480
your time anymore. You'll only bill for

00:05:53.000 --> 00:05:58.800
your taste. [music] So, this swarm gives

00:05:55.480 --> 00:06:01.400
you an army of powerful interns. But,

00:05:58.800 --> 00:06:03.680
your next strategy isn't about hiring AI

00:06:01.400 --> 00:06:05.720
to take orders. [music] It's there to

00:06:03.680 --> 00:06:08.560
challenge you. Your third strategy,

00:06:05.720 --> 00:06:11.680
then, is the devil's advocate. Around

00:06:08.560 --> 00:06:14.600
the 1500s, the Catholic Church was the

00:06:11.680 --> 00:06:17.200
most powerful organization on Earth.

00:06:14.600 --> 00:06:20.640
But, it was also under a lot of attack.

00:06:17.200 --> 00:06:23.360
By 1587, the Pope had a massive problem.

00:06:20.640 --> 00:06:25.640
The Church was canonizing saints left

00:06:23.360 --> 00:06:27.120
and right. So, the Pope did something

00:06:25.640 --> 00:06:29.320
pretty radical.

00:06:27.120 --> 00:06:32.440
He created an office called the

00:06:29.320 --> 00:06:35.640
Advocatus Diaboli, [music] the devil's

00:06:32.440 --> 00:06:37.589
advocate. This person's entire job was

00:06:35.640 --> 00:06:40.400
to argue against the canonization

00:06:37.589 --> 00:06:43.080
[music] of any saint, to find the dirt,

00:06:40.400 --> 00:06:45.080
to poke holes, to be ruthless. The

00:06:43.080 --> 00:06:47.400
Church [music] realized this 400 years

00:06:45.080 --> 00:06:50.000
ago. When the stakes are high, good

00:06:47.400 --> 00:06:52.600
decisions are rarely made through

00:06:50.000 --> 00:06:54.680
agreements. They come from conflict.

00:06:52.600 --> 00:06:57.280
Now, why is this necessary? Well, it's

00:06:54.680 --> 00:06:59.840
called the IKEA effect. We fall in love

00:06:57.280 --> 00:07:01.919
with anything we build ourselves.

00:06:59.840 --> 00:07:03.760
Research shows that smart people are

00:07:01.919 --> 00:07:06.480
great at spotting [music] flaws in other

00:07:03.760 --> 00:07:08.919
people's thinking, physically incapable

00:07:06.480 --> 00:07:12.120
of seeing them in [music] their own. So,

00:07:08.919 --> 00:07:13.840
the key insight? Most people use ChatGPT

00:07:12.120 --> 00:07:17.240
for validation.

00:07:13.840 --> 00:07:18.854
But, you should use it for violation.

00:07:17.240 --> 00:07:21.160
But, here's the problem. ChatGPT's

00:07:18.854 --> 00:07:23.240
[music] programmed to keep you happy.

00:07:21.160 --> 00:07:25.720
So, you have to force it to behave

00:07:23.240 --> 00:07:28.440
differently. So, here are the three

00:07:25.720 --> 00:07:30.520
tricks to break the AI's people-pleaser

00:07:28.440 --> 00:07:33.640
programming. First, use [music] magic

00:07:30.520 --> 00:07:35.800
triggers. Force adversarial thinking.

00:07:33.640 --> 00:07:38.400
Instead of saying, "What do you think?"

00:07:35.800 --> 00:07:40.200
be specific. Prompt it and say, "Audit

00:07:38.400 --> 00:07:43.560
this using first [music] principles."

00:07:40.200 --> 00:07:45.840
Or, "Act as my fiercest competitor." You

00:07:43.560 --> 00:07:47.640
may not get what you want, but you will

00:07:45.840 --> 00:07:49.240
get what [music] you need. Second,

00:07:47.640 --> 00:07:51.880
shadowboxing.

00:07:49.240 --> 00:07:53.240
Don't step into the ring unprepared.

00:07:51.880 --> 00:07:55.760
Let's say if you're going out to raise

00:07:53.240 --> 00:07:58.600
money for your venture. Tell the AI

00:07:55.760 --> 00:08:01.800
simulate a debate between a ruthless

00:07:58.600 --> 00:08:04.280
skeptical VC and a visionary product

00:08:01.800 --> 00:08:07.280
designer. Let the VC push back on the

00:08:04.280 --> 00:08:10.640
idea. I want to see that fight and learn

00:08:07.280 --> 00:08:13.220
from it. This way you let AI expose the

00:08:10.640 --> 00:08:13.240
weak points so you don't fumble on them

00:08:13.220 --> 00:08:15.840
>> [music]

00:08:13.240 --> 00:08:18.280
>> in the real world meeting. Third, the

00:08:15.840 --> 00:08:20.320
blind spot check. Feed it your strategy

00:08:18.280 --> 00:08:23.040
and ask one question. Based on what you

00:08:20.320 --> 00:08:26.520
know about me, what are the gaps in my

00:08:23.040 --> 00:08:29.400
reasoning? When we get successful, we

00:08:26.520 --> 00:08:30.250
develop two bad habits. We trap

00:08:29.400 --> 00:08:30.320
ourselves

00:08:30.250 --> 00:08:33.800
>> [music]

00:08:30.320 --> 00:08:36.240
>> in this bubble of our ego and we

00:08:33.800 --> 00:08:38.719
surround ourselves with people who just

00:08:36.240 --> 00:08:41.880
say yes. That's why this strategy is so

00:08:38.719 --> 00:08:44.000
critical to save you from yourself. But

00:08:41.880 --> 00:08:46.240
knowing where you're wrong or why you're

00:08:44.000 --> 00:08:49.040
wrong is not enough. You still have to

00:08:46.240 --> 00:08:50.880
learn fast about what's right. That's

00:08:49.040 --> 00:08:53.520
where our next strategy comes in, the

00:08:50.880 --> 00:08:56.040
neural link. And it breaks the rule most

00:08:53.520 --> 00:08:58.040
of us grew up believing that mastery

00:08:56.040 --> 00:08:59.680
takes 10,000 hours.

00:08:58.040 --> 00:09:02.400
You know, there is a famous story about

00:08:59.680 --> 00:09:05.280
a physicist, Max Planck. After he won

00:09:02.400 --> 00:09:07.000
the Nobel Prize, he went on a tour

00:09:05.280 --> 00:09:09.640
giving the same speech about quantum

00:09:07.000 --> 00:09:11.760
mechanics every night. Now his chauffeur

00:09:09.640 --> 00:09:14.000
sat in the front row every time and

00:09:11.760 --> 00:09:15.640
literally memorized every word. One

00:09:14.000 --> 00:09:17.400
night the chauffeur said, "Professor,

00:09:15.640 --> 00:09:19.320
I'm getting bored just listening to you.

00:09:17.400 --> 00:09:21.240
Let's switch. I'll give the lecture on

00:09:19.320 --> 00:09:23.200
stage and you sit in the audience

00:09:21.240 --> 00:09:26.800
wearing my cap." Planck of course

00:09:23.200 --> 00:09:29.600
agreed. Same words, same structure, same

00:09:26.800 --> 00:09:32.120
cadence. The speech was flawless, but at

00:09:29.600 --> 00:09:34.760
the end a physicist in the audience

00:09:32.120 --> 00:09:37.120
stood up and asked a deep technical

00:09:34.760 --> 00:09:39.480
question. But the chauffeur didn't panic

00:09:37.120 --> 00:09:42.240
at all. He smiled and said, "I am

00:09:39.480 --> 00:09:44.600
surprised to hear such a simple question

00:09:42.240 --> 00:09:46.760
in a city as advanced as Munich. To

00:09:44.600 --> 00:09:48.400
prove how simple it is, I'm going to let

00:09:46.760 --> 00:09:50.720
my chauffeur answer it." Of course, that

00:09:48.400 --> 00:09:53.480
was Max Planck. But here's the point.

00:09:50.720 --> 00:09:55.240
Most people use ChatGPT like that

00:09:53.480 --> 00:09:57.366
chauffeur. They copy-paste

00:09:55.240 --> 00:09:59.320
smart-sounding phrases and answers

00:09:57.366 --> 00:10:02.040
[music] to sound intelligent. They have

00:09:59.320 --> 00:10:04.440
fluency without foundation. So here are

00:10:02.040 --> 00:10:06.440
three ways to get that Neuralink true

00:10:04.440 --> 00:10:09.320
understanding without becoming that

00:10:06.440 --> 00:10:11.640
chauffeur. First, the jargon translator.

00:10:09.320 --> 00:10:14.560
For instance, tell it, "Explain the

00:10:11.640 --> 00:10:17.320
concept of API using the restaurant

00:10:14.560 --> 00:10:19.760
metaphors only." Suddenly, the menu

00:10:17.320 --> 00:10:22.080
becomes the documentation, the kitchen

00:10:19.760 --> 00:10:24.600
is the server, and the waiter is the

00:10:22.080 --> 00:10:26.760
API, the messenger that takes your

00:10:24.600 --> 00:10:28.760
order, delivers it in the kitchen,

00:10:26.760 --> 00:10:30.400
brings back the results. You aren't

00:10:28.760 --> 00:10:33.440
learning from scratch, you're

00:10:30.400 --> 00:10:35.880
translating that jargon into your

00:10:33.440 --> 00:10:38.520
jargon. Second, the progressive

00:10:35.880 --> 00:10:40.520
professor. Richard Feynman said that if

00:10:38.520 --> 00:10:42.920
you can't explain it simply, you don't

00:10:40.520 --> 00:10:45.960
understand it. So don't ask ChatGPT to

00:10:42.920 --> 00:10:48.680
explain quantum computing, but instead

00:10:45.960 --> 00:10:51.040
say, "Explain quantum computing to me

00:10:48.680 --> 00:10:53.040
three ways. One, like I am a

00:10:51.040 --> 00:10:55.440
five-year-old. Give me the simplest

00:10:53.040 --> 00:10:58.160
explanation. Number two, like I am a

00:10:55.440 --> 00:11:00.480
15-year-old. Explain it with logic and

00:10:58.160 --> 00:11:02.760
examples. And three, like I'm a college

00:11:00.480 --> 00:11:05.160
student. Share the underlying theory."

00:11:02.760 --> 00:11:08.120
When you do that in that particular

00:11:05.160 --> 00:11:10.600
order, you will feel any concept click

00:11:08.120 --> 00:11:13.440
into place in your brain instantly. And

00:11:10.600 --> 00:11:16.760
third, the degree download. You teach

00:11:13.440 --> 00:11:19.440
it, you keep it. So use AI to be your

00:11:16.760 --> 00:11:21.600
target audience or a peer. Prompt it to

00:11:19.440 --> 00:11:24.240
say something like, "I want to build a

00:11:21.600 --> 00:11:26.040
cool AI commerce product. I need to

00:11:24.240 --> 00:11:28.240
learn the foundation of how machine

00:11:26.040 --> 00:11:30.640
learning works. Skip the theory, only

00:11:28.240 --> 00:11:32.960
give me actionable skills, no fluff, and

00:11:30.640 --> 00:11:35.560
then test me so I can teach it back to

00:11:32.960 --> 00:11:37.160
you. You just replaced a boot camp worth

00:11:35.560 --> 00:11:39.720
$50,000

00:11:37.160 --> 00:11:42.320
with a PDF. So, here you are. You have

00:11:39.720 --> 00:11:44.640
built a high-performance ChatGPT

00:11:42.320 --> 00:11:47.320
machine. But, such machines tend to

00:11:44.640 --> 00:11:50.360
overheat. The fifth strategy is called

00:11:47.320 --> 00:11:51.993
the executive coach, and without it none

00:11:50.360 --> 00:11:52.200
of the other strategies would work.

00:11:51.993 --> 00:11:55.680
>> [music]

00:11:52.200 --> 00:11:58.320
>> 2,000 years ago, Marcus Aurelius was the

00:11:55.680 --> 00:12:00.508
emperor of Rome, but he was also the

00:11:58.320 --> 00:12:04.200
loneliest. He had no one to talk,

00:12:00.508 --> 00:12:06.840
[music] so he hired a coach. That coach

00:12:04.200 --> 00:12:09.023
was a blank scroll. Every night in his

00:12:06.840 --> 00:12:09.880
tent, he wrote down his anxieties,

00:12:09.023 --> 00:12:12.760
[music]

00:12:09.880 --> 00:12:16.200
his fears, his advice to himself. He

00:12:12.760 --> 00:12:18.480
titled that manuscript to himself. If

